1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A proposition has a truth value. A predicate does not — until its variable is given one.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Write P(x) for "x is greater than 5". Then P(2) is false, P(7) is true, P(9) is true.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The domain is the set of values the variable may take. It is part of the meaning.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Bind the variable with a quantifier and the predicate is a proposition again.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
For all x, P(x) — written with an upside-down A — means every element of the domain satisfies P.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Over a finite domain it is just a conjunction: P(a) and P(b) and P(c).

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:53,000
One false case refutes it. Here P(b) is false, so for-all is false.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:02,000
On the empty domain there is nothing to check, so for-all is vacuously true.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:09,000
There exists x with P(x) — written with a backwards E — means at least one element satisfies P.

10
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Over a finite domain it is a disjunction: P(a) or P(b) or P(c).

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:25,000
One true case is enough. That element is called a witness.

12
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:34,000
On the empty domain there is no witness, so there-exists is false.

13
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Truth in first-order logic is always relative to a domain and an interpretation.

14
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Take: there exists x such that x is even and x is prime. On {2,4,6} it is true.

15
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:57,000
The same formula on {9,15,21} is false: every element fails the first predicate.

16
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
So a quantified formula is never true on its own — the model comes first.

17
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Put NOT in front of a quantifier and the quantifier flips.

18
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:19,000
NOT for-all is there-exists NOT: not (for all x, P(x)) means some x fails P.

19
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:27,000
NOT there-exists is for-all NOT: no x has P means every x lacks P.

20
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
"Not all that glitters is gold" becomes: some x glitters and is not gold.

21
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Both laws hold on every model — they are equivalences, not rules of thumb.

22
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Two variables, two quantifiers — now the order is part of the meaning.

23
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:56,000
For all x there exists y: each row needs some filled cell, and y may differ per row.

24
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:06,000
There exists y for all x: one single column must be filled all the way down.

25
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Here the first is true and the second false: the three witnesses are all different.

26
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:22,000
So for-all-there-exists does not imply there-exists-for-all.

27
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Two quantifiers of the same kind may be swapped freely.

28
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
for-all x for-all y equals for-all y for-all x, and the same for there-exists.

29
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
The mixed pair implies one way only: there-exists-for-all implies for-all-there-exists.

30
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Of the 512 relations on three elements, 169 satisfy there-exists-for-all and 343 satisfy for-all-there-exists.

31
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Every restricted English sentence has one right shape.

32
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,000
For ALL, restrict with an implication: for all x, student implies studies.

33
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
For SOME, restrict with a conjunction: there exists x who is a student and studies.

34
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Swap them and both sentences break on the same domain.

35
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,000
The four verdicts on this one domain are computed, not assumed.

36
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
A quantifier binds its variable inside its scope only.

37
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,000
In for-all x (P(x) and Q(y)), x is bound and y is free.

38
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:48,000
No free variables means a sentence — it has a truth value in a model.

39
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
One: for-all is a big AND, there-exists is a big OR.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Two: NOT flips the quantifier and negates the body.

41
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Three: mixed order is not swappable — only there-exists-for-all implies for-all-there-exists.

